              "tool_use_id": "toolu_015ixsNd162kHgeoxSXzLb8u",
              "type": "tool_result",
              "content": "116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{Classifier design justification.}\n118\tThe OBD formulation is motivated by a three-way comparison with multiclass LightGBM and ordinal logistic regression (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n119\tAcross all three model families, OBD achieves the best accuracy (38.2--42.3\\%, i.e., $+$21.5--25.6\\,pp over the 16.7\\% random baseline for 6-class classification, and $+$5.5\\,pp over frontier-LLM zero-shot on the matched 5-class task, 38.6\\% vs.\\ ${\\sim}$33\\%; cf.\\ Table~\\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}) while maintaining competitive MAE.\n120\tOrdinal logistic regression yields the lowest MAE (e.g., 0.911 vs.\\ 0.984 OBD on Qwen-rewrite) but at the cost of near-chance pairwise AUC (mean 0.49 vs.\\ 0.61 for OBD across families)---it reduces error by collapsing predictions toward central depths rather than genuinely distinguishing adjacent levels.\n121\tMulticlass LightGBM matches OBD on pairwise AUC but consistently trails in both accuracy and MAE.\n122\tOBD thus provides the best balance across all three evaluation axes, justifying its use as the default classifier throughout.\n123\t\n124\t%% ====================================================================\n125\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}"
            }
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "parentUuid": "260cb730-bba8-4cbc-aad0-799e0776a181",
            "isSidechain": false,
            "promptId": "bcde7801-583c-40a9-be90-35ab0efe12b0",
            "type": "user",
            "uuid": "bb0c6d38-2331-4f93-b957-4acd499ab514",
            "toolUseResult": {
              "type": "text",
              "file": {
                "filePath": "/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_7/projects/gen-depth-contamination/docs/paper/experiments_c1.tex",
                "content": "\n\\paragraph{Classifier design justification.}\nThe OBD formulation is motivated by a three-way comparison with multiclass LightGBM and ordinal logistic regression (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\nAcross all three model families, OBD achieves the best accuracy (38.2--42.3\\%, i.e., $+$21.5--25.6\\,pp over the 16.7\\% random baseline for 6-class classification, and $+$5.5\\,pp over frontier-LLM zero-shot on the matched 5-class task, 38.6\\% vs.\\ ${\\sim}$33\\%; cf.\\ Table~\\ref{tab:s3_llm_zeroshot}) while maintaining competitive MAE.\nOrdinal logistic regression yields the lowest MAE (e.g., 0.911 vs.\\ 0.984 OBD on Qwen-rewrite) but at the cost of near-chance pairwise AUC (mean 0.49 vs.\\ 0.61 for OBD across families)---it reduces error by collapsing predictions toward central depths rather than genuinely distinguishing adjacent levels.\nMulticlass LightGBM matches OBD on pairwise AUC but consistently trails in both accuracy and MAE.\nOBD thus provides the best balance across all three evaluation axes, justifying its use as the default classifier throughout.\n\n%% ====================================================================\n\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}",
                "numLines": 10,
                "startLine": 116,
                "totalLines": 410
              }
            },
